  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\u2207f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i+y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\u2207f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(-1,1) & =-\\,i+j \\\\ \u2207f(2,2) & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(2,-1) & =2\\,i+(-1)j=2\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png)",
